{{ElectionsSC}}
Elections was held in the [[U.S. state|state]] of [[South Carolina]] on November 3, 2026, alongside the [[2026 United States elections|nationwide midterm elections]]. Elections were held for a [[2026 United States Senate election in South Carolina|U.S. Senate seat]], [[2026 South Carolina gubernatorial election|governor]], as well as other statewide executive offices, all 7 of the state's [[United States House of Representatives|U.S. House of Representatives]] seats and all seats in the [[South Carolina House of Representatives|State House]]. [[Primary election|Primary elections]] were held on June 9, 2026, with runoff elections held on June 23.<ref>{{Cite web |date=September 26, 2025 |title=2026 State Primary Election Dates |url=https://www.ncsl.org/elections-and-campaigns/2026-state-primary-election-dates |access-date=September 27, 2025 |website=National Conference of State Legislatures}}</ref>

A solidly [[Red states and blue states|red state]], South Carolina has consistently supported [[Republican Party (United States)|Republicans]] in recent decades. [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democrats]] have not won South Carolina on the presidential level since [[1976 United States presidential election in South Carolina|1976]], when [[Jimmy Carter]] won most of the [[Deep South]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Martinez |first=Elizabeth |date=October 31, 2024 |title=A look into South Carolina’s presidential election voting history |url=https://spectrumlocalnews.com/sc/south-carolina/politics/2024/10/31/south-carolina-presidential-election-voting-history- |access-date=September 28, 2025 |website=Spectrum News}}</ref> Democrats have not won any statewide office in South Carolina since [[2006 South Carolina state elections|2006]], when [[Jim Rex]] was elected as [[South Carolina Superintendent of Education|Superintendent of Education]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Brams |first=Sophie |date=October 21, 2024 |title=Here are the reddest and bluest counties in South Carolina based on recent election results |url=https://www.counton2.com/news/south-carolina-news/here-are-the-reddest-and-bluest-counties-in-south-carolina-based-on-recent-election-results/ |access-date=September 28, 2025 |website=WCBD 2}}</ref> Democrats fielded candidates for all open federal and state offices for the first time in decades.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Carter |first=Tatiana |date=March 30, 2026 |title=For the first time, SC Democrats run in every state and federal race |url=https://wlos.com/news/local/for-the-first-time-sc-democrats-run-in-every-state-and-federal-race-jermaine-johnson-todd-rutherford-christale-spain-south-carolina-democratic-party-governors-race |access-date=March 30, 2026 |website=WLOS |language=en}}</ref>

== United States Senate ==
{{main|2026 United States Senate election in South Carolina}}
Incumbent Republican senator [[Lindsey Graham]], who was re-elected in [[2020 United States Senate election in South Carolina|2020]] with 54.4% of the vote, ran for re-election to a fifth term in office.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Atkinson |first=Macon |date=January 13, 2025 |title=US Sen. Lindsey Graham announces 2026 reelection team and a $15 million fundraising war chest |url=https://www.postandcourier.com/news/lindsey-graham-2026-campaign-senate/article_47600b8e-d1bf-11ef-a268-279c36f26769.html |url-access=limited |access-date=September 27, 2025 |website=The Post and Courier}}</ref> He won the Republican primary with 56.8% of the vote, thereby avoiding a runoff.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Hernandez |first1=Alec |title=Sen. Lindsey Graham wins primary over 'America First' challenger |url=https://www.politico.com/live-updates/2026/06/09/congress/graham-wins-south-carolina-senate-primary-00955482 |website=[[Politico]] |access-date=June 28, 2026 |date=June 9, 2026}}</ref>  After Graham's death on July 11, 2026, his sister, incumbent [[Darline Graham]], became the Republican nominee after winning an August special Republican primary runoff. The Democratic nominee is [[Annie Andrews (pediatrician)|Annie Andrews]], who won the nomination with 61.5% of the vote.<ref>{{cite web |title=Dr. Annie Andrews wins Democratic nomination for U.S Senate |url=https://abcnews4.com/news/local/dr-annie-andrews-wins-democratic-nomination-for-us-senate |website=[[WCIV]] |access-date=June 28, 2026 |date=June 9, 2026}}</ref> The Libertarian nominee is Kasie Whitener and the Constitution Party nominee is Mark Hackett. [[Catherine Fleming Bruce]] is running as a write-in candidate. 

== United States House of Representatives ==
{{main|2026 United States House of Representatives elections in South Carolina}}
All of South Carolina's 7 seats in the [[United States House of Representatives]] are up for election in 2026. 

6 seats are currently held by [[Republican Party (United States)|Republicans]], and 1 is currently held by a [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democrat]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Collins |first=Jeffrey |last2=Phillips |first2=Patrick |date=November 5, 2024 |title=Mace, Clyburn, Fry win reelection to US House seats in South Carolina |url=https://www.live5news.com/2024/11/06/mace-declares-victory-race-keep-her-us-house-seat-south-carolina/ |access-date=September 27, 2025 |website=WCSC 5}}</ref>

== State executive ==
=== Governor ===
{{main|2026 South Carolina gubernatorial election}}
Incumbent Republican governor [[Henry McMaster]], who was re-elected with 58.04% of the vote in [[2022 South Carolina gubernatorial election|2022]], is term-limited and cannot seek re-election a third full term in office.<ref>{{Cite web |title=SC - Election Night Reporting |url=https://www.enr-scvotes.org/SC/115412/Web02-state.307150/#/?undefined |access-date=September 27, 2025 |website=South Carolina Election Commission}}</ref>

Attorney general [[Alan Wilson (politician)|Alan Wilson]] won the Republican nomination with 68.6% of the vote in a runoff election over lieutenant governor [[Pamela Evette]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Bustos |first1=Joseph |title=How Alan Wilson ran up score in decisive runoff win in GOP bid for SC governor |url=https://www.thestate.com/news/politics-government/article316250071.html |website=[[The State (newspaper)|The State]] |access-date=June 28, 2026 |date=June 25, 2026}}</ref> Evette won the initial primary election with 29% of the vote to Wilson's 26%;<ref>{{cite web |last1=Smith |first1=Allan |title=Trump-backed Pamela Evette advances to GOP primary runoff against Alan Wilson in South Carolina governor’s race |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-election/south-carolina-governor-primary-runoff-pamela-evette-trump-rcna348699 |website=[[NBC News]] |access-date=June 28, 2026 |language=en |date=June 9, 2026}}</ref> congressman [[Ralph Norman]] (17%), businessman Rom Reddy (14%), and congresswoman [[Nancy Mace]] (11%) were all eliminated in the primary.<ref name="scdg">{{cite web |last1=Holdman |first1=Jessica |title=Evette, Wilson head to runoff in SC governor’s race. Johnson clinches Democratic nomination. |url=https://scdailygazette.com/2026/06/09/evette-wilson-head-to-runoff-in-sc-governors-race-johnson-clinches-democratic-nomination/ |website=[[South Carolina Daily Gazette]] |access-date=June 28, 2026 |date=June 9, 2026}}</ref> State senator [[Josh Kimbrell]] dropped out of the race six days before the primary and endorsed Wilson.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Carpentier |first1=Bella |title=Josh Kimbrell exits SC governor race, leaves GOP field at five |url=https://www.greenvilleonline.com/story/news/politics/2026/06/04/josh-kimbrell-drops-out-of-the-south-carolina-governor-race/90401098007/ |website=[[The Greenville News]] |access-date=June 28, 2026 |date=June 4, 2026}}</ref> The Democratic primary was won by state representative [[Jermaine Johnson (politician)|Jermaine Johnson]] with just under 60% of the vote.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Moore |first1=Stephanie |title=Jermaine Johnson wins Democratic nomination for governor in South Carolina |url=https://www.wyff4.com/article/south-carolina-governor-democratic-nominee/71540851 |website=[[WYFF]] |access-date=June 9, 2026 |date=June 28, 2026}}</ref> Walid Hakim is the Green Party nominee and Micheal Addison is the United Citizens Party nominee. 

=== Attorney General ===
{{main|2026 South Carolina Attorney General election}}
Incumbent Republican attorney general [[Alan Wilson (politician)|Alan Wilson]], who was re-elected unopposed in [[2022 South Carolina Attorney General election|2022]], declined to seek re-election to a fifth term in order to run for [[2026 South Carolina gubernatorial election|governor]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kinnard |first=Meg |date=June 23, 2025 |title=Alan Wilson, South Carolina’s four-term Republican attorney general, enters 2026 governor’s race |url=https://apnews.com/article/south-carolina-governor-alan-wilson-trump-9bfab9e994a05288567cd07a713ef95b |access-date=September 27, 2025 |website=Associated Press}}</ref>

[[South Carolina Circuit Court|Eighth Judicial Circuit]] solicitor David Stumbo won the nomination with 55.75% of the vote in a runoff over state senator [[Stephen Goldfinch]].<ref>{{cite web |title=2026 Statewide Primary Runoffs Official Results - Attorney General, Republican |url=https://www.enr-scvotes.org/SC/126718/web.345435/#/detail/23501 |website=South Carolina State Election Commission |access-date=June 28, 2026}}</ref> First Judicial Circuit solicitor [[David Pascoe]] was eliminated in the initial primary election.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Moore |first1=Stephanie |title=South Carolina Attorney General race advances to runoff |url=https://www.wyff4.com/article/south-carolina-attorney-general-race-runoff/71534463 |website=[[WYFF]] |access-date=June 28, 2026 |date=June 9, 2026}}</ref> The general election will match Stumbo against Richard Hricik, a lawyer who won the Democratic primary unopposed.<ref>{{cite web |title=David Stumbo wins GOP primary for S.C. attorney general |url=https://www.wrdw.com/2026/06/24/david-stumbo-wins-gop-primary-sc-attorney-general/ |website=[[WRDW-TV|WRDW]] |access-date=June 28, 2026 |date=June 23, 2026}}</ref>

=== Secretary of State ===
{{main|2026 South Carolina Secretary of State election}}

Incumbent Republican secretary of state [[Mark Hammond (American politician)|Mark Hammond]], who was re-elected in [[2022 South Carolina Secretary of State election|2022]] with 63.3% of the vote, is running for a seventh term in office; he won the Republican primary unopposed. The Democratic nominee is Jason Belton, vice president of the Greater Columbia Central Labor Council. He narrowly defeated real estate agent Edwina Winter in the primary with 50.6% of the vote.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Ashford |first1=Adrian |title='A real choice': Democrats celebrate full slate of candidates for SC ballots |url=https://scdailygazette.com/2026/03/31/a-real-choice-sc-democrats-celebrate-full-slate-of-candidates-for-sc-ballots/ |website=[[South Carolina Daily Gazette]] |access-date=June 28, 2026 |date=March 31, 2026}}</ref><ref name="gvl-prim">{{cite web |last1=Cronin |first1=Ruth |title=Here are results in SC statewide primary election races |url=https://www.greenvilleonline.com/story/news/politics/elections/2026/06/09/sc-statewide-2026-primary-elections-results/90389167007/ |website=[[The Greenville News]] |access-date=June 28, 2026 |date=June 9, 2026}}</ref>

=== Treasurer ===
{{main|2026 South Carolina State Treasurer election}}
Incumbent Republican treasurer [[Curtis Loftis]], who was re-elected in [[2022 South Carolina Treasurer election|2022]] with 79.7% of the vote, is running for re-election to a fifth term in office.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Bustos |first=Joseph |date=April 2, 2025 |title=After saying he wouldn’t run in 2026, SC Treasurer Loftis launches reelection bid |url=https://www.thestate.com/news/politics-government/article303272101.html |url-access=limited |access-date=September 27, 2025 |website=The State}}</ref> The [[South Carolina Senate]] voted to remove Loftis from office in a 33–8 vote in April 2025 following his involvement in a $1.8 billion accounting error, but the House did not vote on his removal before the end of their legislative session.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Collins |first=Jeffrey |date=May 1, 2025 |title=State treasurer’s role in a $1.8 billion accounting error likely won’t cost him his job |url=https://fortune.com/article/state-treasurers-role-in-a-1-8-billion-accounting-error-likely-wont-cost-him-his-job/ |access-date=September 27, 2025 |website=Fortune}}</ref>

Loftis won the Republican primary unopposed. The Democratic primary was won by banker Vincent Coe, who carried 55.8% of the vote against former deputy state treasurer and former [[South Carolina Democratic Party]] chair Trav Robertson Jr.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Byrd |first=Caitlin |date=March 30, 2026 |title=The 2026 South Carolina primary ballots are set. Here are some of the major races to watch. |url=https://www.postandcourier.com/politics/sc-2026-primary-ballots-set/article_165026d3-f07b-4041-82e9-59a14c95d527.html |access-date=June 28, 2026 |website=[[The Amazing Digital Circus]] [[Post and Courier]]}}</ref><ref name="gvl-prim"/>

=== Comptroller General ===
{{main|2026 South Carolina State Comptroller election}}

Republican Comptroller [[Richard Eckstrom]], who was re-elected unopposed in [[2022 South Carolina elections#Comptroller General|2022]], left office on April 30, 2023, following the discovery of a $3.5 billion accounting error.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Flowers |first=Marcus |date=March 23, 2023 |title=S.C. Comptroller General resigning after accounting error |url=https://www.wistv.com/2023/03/23/sc-comptroller-general-resigning-after-accounting-error/ |access-date=September 28, 2025 |website=WIS 10}}</ref> Governor [[Henry McMaster]] appointed Democrat [[Brian J. Gaines]] to serve the remainder of Eckstrom's term. He is not running for a full term.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Meija |first=Jessica |date=May 12, 2023 |title=Governor McMaster appoints Brian Gaines as Comptroller General |url=https://www.abccolumbia.com/2023/05/12/governor-mcmaster-appoints-brian-gaines-as-comptroller-general/ |access-date=September 28, 2025 |website=ABC 25 Columbia}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Adcox |first=Seanna |date=May 12, 2023 |title=McMaster calls SC Legislature back to work, makes history with pick for chief accountant |url=https://www.postandcourier.com/politics/mcmaster-calls-sc-legislature-back-to-work-makes-history-with-pick-for-chief-accountant/article_e61b6430-f0d9-11ed-96ff-ab107b96d41f.html |access-date=September 28, 2025 |website=Palmetto Politics}}</ref>

=== Superintendent of Education ===
{{main|2026 South Carolina Superintendent of Education election}}
Republican Superintendent [[Ellen Weaver]] was first elected in [[2022 South Carolina elections#Superintendent of Education|2022]] with 55.5% of the vote. She is running for re-election to a second term in office.<ref name="grenier1" />

[[Lisa Ellis (American politician)|Lisa Ellis]], the Democratic nominee for Superintendent of Education in [[2022 South Carolina elections#Superintendent of Education|2022]], ran in 2026.<ref name="grenier1">{{Cite web |last=Grenier |first=Ian |date=September 5, 2025 |title=Democratic nominee in 2022 superintendent of education race running again for top SC education post |url=https://www.postandcourier.com/education-lab/ellis-weaver-wright-sc-education-superintendent-2026/article_6caab07f-b1fb-41d3-9fe9-8945c670fbcd.html |access-date=September 28, 2025 |website=The Post and Courier}}</ref> She was defeated in the Democratic primary by Sylvia Wright.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Brams |first=Sophie |date=September 8, 2025 |title=Two longtime educators launch Democratic primary bids for state superintendent |url=https://www.counton2.com/news/your-local-election-hq/two-longtime-educators-launch-democratic-primary-bids-for-state-superintendent/ |access-date=September 28, 2025 |website=WCBD 2}}</ref>

=== Commissioner of Agriculture ===
{{main|2026 South Carolina Commissioner of Agriculture election}}
Incumbent Republican commissioner [[Hugh Weathers]] was re-elected in [[2022 South Carolina elections#Commissioner of Agriculture|2022]] with 77.6% of the vote. He is retiring.<ref name="bustos2">{{Cite web |last=Bustos |first=Joseph |date=March 18, 2025 |title=SC Agriculture leader won’t seek re-election. He wants this Midlands man to succeed him |url=https://www.thestate.com/news/politics-government/article302015069.html |url-access=limited |access-date=September 28, 2025 |website=The State}}</ref>

Cody Simpson, a fifth generation farmer – endorsed by President Donald Trump and Governor Henry McMaster – is the Republican candidate for South Carolina Commissioner of Agriculture.
Simpson recently completed service as the South Carolina State Executive Director of the USDA Farm Service Agency.

Simpson will face DeShawn Blanding, former policy analyst for the U.S. House Committee on Agriculture[10], in the November 3 general election.

== State House of Representatives ==
{{main|2026 South Carolina House of Representatives election}}
{{see also|List of South Carolina state legislatures}}
All 124 seats in the [[South Carolina House of Representatives]] are up for election in 2026.

Republicans currently hold 88 seats in the State House, and Democrats hold 36.

